# help texts for `udiksa <command> -h` (read by ~/.local/bin/udiksa). Format per command:
#   [name]  /  first line = one-line description  /  usage: <arguments>  /  "# HEADING" starts a table
#   "  label  description" = a table row (two spaces between)  /  note: text  /  example: command  explanation
[theme]
Themes: a folder of wallpapers is one palette that recolours the whole rice.
usage: <command> [arguments]
# COMMANDS
  next | prev            Next / previous wallpaper of this theme
  set <name>             A theme by name, any case (--transition grow|wipe|none)
  random [filter]        A random picture: dark, light, or wallpaper (this theme)
  apply <picture>        That wallpaper and its theme everywhere (--pos X,Y)
  names [dark|light]     Theme names, one per line
  current                The theme and wallpaper in use (JSON)
  list                   All themes for the switcher (JSON)
  reapply                Write the current theme again (after editing colors.toml)
  colors <picture>       Show a picture's main colours
  import <github link>   A community theme repo with colors.toml and pictures
  corners                Redo the rounded corners of the lock, login and GRUB screens
  thumbs                 Remake the previews
note: A theme is a folder in ~/Pictures/Wallpapers/: new folder = new theme. Its colours come from colors.toml in that folder.
example: udiksa theme set "Tokyo Night"  switch theme
example: udiksa theme random dark  a surprise, dark only
[wallpaper]
Change the wallpaper (same theme, or a picture of your own).
usage: next | prev | random | set <picture> | current
# COMMANDS
  next | prev            Next / previous wallpaper of the current theme
  random                 A random wallpaper of the current theme
  set <picture>          Use this picture (its folder decides the theme)
  current                Print the path of the wallpaper in use
example: udiksa wallpaper random  a new look in the same theme
[cursor]
The theme-coloured mouse pointers (Udiksa, Udiksa-Modern).
usage: build [Udiksa|Udiksa-Modern]
usage: greeter <theme> <size>
# COMMANDS
  build [name]           Rebuild the pointer from the theme colours (no name = the one in use)
  greeter <theme> <size> Copy a pointer to the login screen
note: A theme change rebuilds the pointer by itself. Choose the pointer in Settings > Mouse & keyboard.
[shot]
Screenshots: saved to ~/Pictures/Screenshots, copied, and announced in the notch.
usage: area | window | screen | edit
# COMMANDS
  area                   Drag a rectangle
  window                 Click a window
  screen                 The whole screen
  edit                   Freeze the screen, pick an area, draw on it (arrows, boxes, text, blur)
note: In the editor Enter saves and copies, Esc throws it away.
example: udiksa shot area  select a region
[record]
Screen recording, encoded by the GPU. Run it again (or `stop`) to stop.
usage: screen | region | sound | stop
# COMMANDS
  screen                 The screen the mouse is on
  region                 Pick an area first
  sound                  The screen plus what you hear and the microphone
  stop                   Stop a running recording
note: Saved in ~/Videos/Recordings. The notch shows a blinking dot and the time while it runs.
[pick]
Colour picker: click anywhere, the hex code is copied and shown in the notch.
usage: (no arguments)
note: Esc cancels.
[toggle]
Show or hide a notch panel, Settings or the theme switcher.
usage: <panel>
# PANELS
  battery power volume wifi bluetooth media notifications system
  tailscale launcher usage clock clockTimer clockAlarm clockReminder clockEvent
# OTHER
  themes                 The theme switcher
  settings               The Settings app
example: udiksa toggle system  open or close the system panel
[open]
Open a notch panel (it stays open until you leave it). Close it with `udiksa close`.
usage: <panel>
note: Panels are listed in `udiksa toggle -h`.
[close]
Close the open notch panel.
usage: (no arguments)
[clipboard]
Clipboard history, in the launcher.
usage: (no arguments)
[emoji]
Emoji picker, in the launcher.
usage: (no arguments)
[lock]
Lock the screen.
usage: (no arguments)
[dnd]
Do not disturb: toggles notification pop-ups on and off.
usage: (no arguments)
[osd]
Show a message in the notch.
usage: "<icon>" "<text>"
example: udiksa osd "󰄬" "Backup done"  an icon and a line of text
[scratch]
App scratchpads: each app lives on its own hidden workspace that a key shows and hides.
usage: sysmon | music | chat
# COMMANDS
  sysmon                 btop                                    Ctrl+Shift+Esc
  music                  ncspot + cava, or your own app          Super+M
  chat                   your chat app                           Super+D
note: The first press starts the app, later presses show or hide it. Pick the music and chat apps in Settings > Apps.
[settings]
The back end of the Settings app: saves your choices and applies them live.
usage: get | set <key> <value> | reset [section]
usage: power [<key> <value> | apply]
usage: monitor set <output> '<json>' | many '<json>' | rm <output>
usage: anim <key> <value> | cursor [<theme> <size>] | shake on|off
usage: startup add|rm <command> | bind add <keys> <command> | bind rm <keys>
# COMMANDS
  get                    All current values (JSON)
  set <key> <value>      Save and apply one option
  reset [section]        Forget saved options (all, look, layout or input)
  power                  Lock, screen-off and sleep timers, lid and power-button actions
  monitor                One screen's setup, several at once, or back to automatic
  anim                   Animation speed, styles and feel
  cursor                 Mouse pointer theme and size
  shake on|off           Shake to find the pointer
  startup add|rm         Your own startup commands
  bind add|rm            Your own shortcuts, e.g. "SUPER + B" "zen-browser"
note: Choices are written to ~/.config/hypr/local/ (your personal layer, not in the repo). Use `--full` for every key.
[shell]
Start the Quickshell shell at the right physical size for this screen.
usage: [--restart]
# COMMANDS
  (none)                 Start the shell
  --restart              Stop the running shell and start it again
[ipc]
Call any command of the running shell.
usage: <target> <function> [arguments]
usage: show
# COMMANDS
  show                   List every target and function
note: Targets include notch, themes, settings, notifications, modes, osd, lock, agenda, record, power.
example: udiksa ipc notch open media  open the media panel
[display]
Screens: cycle or set how an external monitor is used (F9).
usage: [extend | mirror | external | laptop]
# COMMANDS
  (none)                 Next layout: extend, mirror, external only, laptop only
  extend                 Use both screens side by side
  mirror                 Show the same picture on both
  external               External screen only
  laptop                 Laptop screen only
note: Without an external screen it just says so.
[touchpad]
Switch the laptop touchpad off or on (F10) and show the state in the notch.
usage: (no arguments)
[panel-hz]
Set the refresh rate of the laptop's own panel; resolution, position and scale stay.
usage: <hz>
example: udiksa panel-hz 60  save power on battery
[gpu]
One-line GPU facts (never wakes the dGPU).
usage: mode | display | dgpu | all
# COMMANDS
  mode                   GPU mode: Integrated, Hybrid or AsusMuxDgpu
  display                Which GPU drives the laptop panel
  dgpu                   NVIDIA GPU power state: active or suspended
  all                    The three above
[idle]
Timed actions of hypridle (set the times in Settings > Power).
usage: off <bat|ac|any> | sleep <bat|ac|any>
# COMMANDS
  off <source>           Screen off, only when on that power source
  sleep <source>         Sleep (then hibernate later, if Settings says so)
[defaults]
Set which app opens which kind of file.
usage: (no arguments)
note: Safe to run again. Written to ~/.config/mimeapps.list, per category on purpose.
[flash]
Write a disk image (.iso, .img, also .xz .gz .zst) to a USB stick or SD card.
usage: [image]
note: Lists only removable drives, asks before writing, can verify afterwards.
[xdpi]
Set the DPI for X11 apps from the screen scale.
usage: (no arguments)
note: Runs at login and after a scale change.
[clock]
Open the terminal clock window, in the theme accent.
usage: (no arguments)
[kbd]
ASUS keyboard lighting (does nothing on other machines).
usage: [set <key> <value>... | apply | theme | lit]
# COMMANDS
  (none)                 Saved choices and what this keyboard can do (JSON)
  set <key> <value>      Save and apply (several pairs are fine)
  apply                  Apply everything saved
  theme                  Re-apply when "follow theme" is on
  lit                    The light was just switched on: send the colour again
# KEYS
  brightness             off | low | med | high
  effect                 static | breathe | rainbow-cycle | rainbow-wave | pulse
  colour                 rrggbb (colour2 = the second colour of breathe)
  follow_theme           true | false
  speed, direction       low|med|high, up|down|left|right
  boot awake sleep shutdown   true | false (when the light is on at all)
example: udiksa kbd set colour ff8800 effect breathe  orange, breathing
[slash]
ASUS lid light (the Slash bar), where the laptop has one.
usage: [set <key> <value>... | apply]
# COMMANDS
  (none)                 Settings and capabilities (JSON)
  set <key> <value>      Change one or more (true/false for the switches)
  apply                  Light or darken the bar for the power source now
[onlyoffice]
OnlyOffice, sized for this screen (it is an X11 app).
usage: [files or --new:word|cell|slide|form]
[install]
Run the installer parts.
usage: [arch] [apps] [rice]
# COMMANDS
  arch                   A blank machine only: wipes the disk you pick (from the Arch USB)
  apps                   Core apps and the optional ones you say yes to
  rice                   Link the dotfiles, login screen, boot splash, hibernation
note: `udiksa install rice` alone is safe to repeat.
[update]
Update the system, or the dotfiles themselves.
usage: [system | dots]
# COMMANDS
  system                 Official packages, AUR packages, Hyprland plugins (default)
  dots                   git pull the Udiksa repo, relink, reload and reapply the theme
[doctor]
Check that everything the rice needs is in place.
usage: (no arguments)
note: Lists missing commands, the Hyprland and shell state, the udiksa commands and dangling links. Exit code 1 if something is missing.
[firstrun]
The setup steps that need a running desktop (runs once after install).
usage: (no arguments)
note: Safe to run by hand at any time. Log: ~/.cache/rice-firstrun.log.
[completion]
Print the bash completion script.
usage: (no arguments)
[version]
The version of this install (the git revision of the Udiksa repo).
usage: (no arguments)
